            <ArticleTitle>DNA polymeric films as a support for cell growth as a new material for regenerative medicine: Compatibility and applicability.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>404-412</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0014-4827(17)30513-X</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.yexcr.2017.09.033</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>DNA polymeric films (DNA-PFs) are a promising drug delivery system (DDS) in modern medicine. In this study, we evaluated the growth behavior of oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC) cells on DNA-PFs. The morphological, biochemical, and cytometric features of OSCC cell adhesion on DNA-PFs were also assessed. An initial, temporary alteration in cell morphology was observed at early time points owing to the inhibition of cell attachment to the film, which then returned to a normal morphological state at later time points. MTT and resazurin assays showed a moderate reduction in cell viability related to increased DNA concentration in the DNA-PFs. Flow cytometry studies showed low cytotoxicity of DNA-PFs, with cell viabilities higher than 90% in all the DNA-PFs tested. Flow cytometric cell cycle analysis also showed average cell cycle phase distributions at later time points, indicating that OSCC cell growth is maintained in the presence of DNA-PFs. These results show high biocompatibility of DNA-PFs and suggest their use in designing &quot;dressing material,&quot; where the DNA film acts as a support for cell growth, or with incorporation of active or photoactive compounds, which can induce tissue regeneration and are useful to treat many diseases, especially oral cancer.</AbstractText>
